\subsection{Explaining Outcomes in Terms of Beliefs and Values}
\label{sec:decomposition}

To distinguish failures attributable to state uncertainty, inappropriate preferences, or action selection, we analyse the belief–action gap $\Delta_{t,p}^{\mathrm{LM}}$ defined in \Cref{sec:belief-action-gap}. 
This measures the consistency of the emitted action with the agent's inferred subjective preferences under its represented state distribution. 
We compute the gap separately in the pre-reasoning and post-reasoning conditions using belief and value estimates derived from the corresponding activations.
% persist despite available state information. If an action is suboptimal under the true state but has a small belief--action gap, it remains compatible with the agent-supervised preference model under the decoded belief. A large gap instead indicates that more highly valued actions remain available even after accounting for uncertainty in the represented state.

