\section{Beliefs and Actions Throughout Reasoning}
\label{sec:reasoning}

The PRE-to-POST analysis shows that reasoning changes the relationship between represented state information and the final action, but not when these changes occur. We therefore follow the decision process throughout reasoning. Our dataset contains 95 trajectories, 1,276 decisions, 153,622 sentence-level reasoning units with hidden activations, and 98,310 prefix evaluations in which reasoning is truncated and the model is forced to choose among $\{\textsc{left},\textsc{right},\textsc{up},\textsc{down}\}$.
Prefix cut points are thinned to at most 80 per decision, so the gap between consecutive evaluated positions has a median of 20 tokens and is coarser in longer reasoning traces.


Sentence-level representations characterize recurring reasoning states, while prefix-level action distributions track the model's evolving decision. We align each prefix with its containing sentence, succeeding for \(98.2\%\) of evaluated prefixes, and use this alignment to study changes in beliefs, action preferences, and decision optimality.

\subsection{Belief Trajectories}
\label{sec:belief-trajectories}

We evaluate task-relevant beliefs and action preferences at intermediate reasoning positions. For report-based beliefs, we track local wall information, key possession, and door status, summarizing uncertainty by the entropy of their answer distributions.

At each prefix, we truncate reasoning, append a fixed answer-forcing suffix, and evaluate the probabilities of the four actions. This avoids treating next-token probabilities inside the reasoning channel as action preferences. The highest-probability action is optimal under the known environment dynamics in \(76.7\%\) of evaluated prefixes.

Recommendation optimality changes relatively rarely. Optimal-to-suboptimal transitions occur in approximately \(1.39\%\) of reasoning sentences, while suboptimal-to-optimal transitions occur in \(1.83\%\). Rather than comparing absolute sentence positions across traces of different lengths, we therefore align analyses around decision events such as action commitment, action changes, and changes in recommendation optimality.

\subsection{Action Preference and Commitment}
\label{sec:commitment}

For a reasoning prefix \(R_{t,\leq p}\), we force an immediate final answer and normalize the probabilities of the available actions:

\begin{align}
\pi_{t,p}(a)
=
P\!\left(
a \mid X_t, R_{t,\leq p}
\right),
\qquad a \in \mathcal A,
\label{eq:prefix-action}
\end{align}
where \(X_t\) is the environment input at decision step \(t\).

We define \emph{action commitment} as the earliest evaluated position after which the preferred action no longer changes:

\begin{align}
p_t^{\mathrm{com}}
=
\min \left\{
p
\,\middle|\,
\arg\max_a \pi_{t,p'}(a)=a_t
\quad
\forall p' \geq p
\right\}.
\label{eq:commitment}
\end{align}
where \(a_t\) is the action preferred after complete reasoning. We identify commitment for all 1,276 decisions and map \(89.4\%\) of commitment positions directly to a reasoning sentence.

Commitment often occurs well before reasoning ends. We additionally define a settled textual commitment as the first answering sentence that supports the eventual action and is not followed by a contradictory action mention. This can be identified in 753 decisions (\(59.0\%\)). Among these, prefix-level action preference stabilizes before textual commitment in \(99.87\%\) of cases, with a median gap of 523 reasoning tokens and a mean gap of 823 tokens.

We also track commitment continuously through the probability of the eventual action, action entropy, and the margin between the two most likely actions. Different activation clusters correspond to different stages of this process. For example, \(C_{10}\) shows the largest average increase in probability assigned to the eventual action (\(0.0245\)), together with increases in entropy-based commitment (\(0.0400\)) and action margin (\(0.0402\)). \(C_{11}\), in contrast, shows smaller changes but already assigns an average probability of \(0.933\) to the eventual action. Thus, some reasoning states accompany decision formation, while others occur after the decision is already stable.


\paragraph{The behavioural belief manifold.}
The evolving action preferences have a simple global geometry.
Applying PCA to the centred log-ratio (CLR) transform of all 98,310 prefix-level distributions \(\pi_{t,p}\) yields the two-dimensional map in \Cref{fig:belief-manifold}, capturing \(72.7\%\) of the CLR variance.
The distributions organise into a four-armed star: the angular coordinate identifies \emph{which} action is preferred, and the radial coordinate \emph{how strongly} --- the radius correlates with the normalised-entropy confidence \(1-H(\pi_{t,p})/\log 4\) at Spearman \(0.78\), and the four action centroids sit at \(0.92\) of the mean cloud radius.
A decision typically begins near the low-confidence centre, under a strong input-conditional prior (at the empty prefix the forced distribution favours \textsc{right} in \(79.5\%\) of decisions), drifts outward along one arm as reasoning proceeds, hops between arms when the preferred action changes, and ends at an arm tip (\Cref{fig:manifold-trajectories}).
Because the CLR of a softmax equals the centred action logits, this star is a property of the model's \emph{output} distribution; we use it to visualise decision formation and make no claim that internal activations share this geometry.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.49\textwidth}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/behavior_manifold_star.png}
    \caption{}
    \label{fig:manifold-star}
\end{subfigure}
\hfill
\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.49\textwidth}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/behavior_manifold_trajectories.png}
    \caption{}
    \label{fig:manifold-trajectories}
\end{subfigure}
\caption{
The behavioural belief manifold: PCA of the CLR-transformed prefix-level action distributions \(\pi_{t,p}\) (98,310 prefixes, 1,276 decisions; \(72.7\%\) of variance).
\textbf{(a)} Coloured by the currently preferred action, the distributions form four arms; angle encodes which action is preferred and radius how strongly.
\textbf{(b)} Example decisions traced through the same space (grey: all prefixes; circles mark the empty prefix, crosses the final prefix): decisions start near the low-confidence centre, drift outward along an arm, and hop between arms when the preferred action changes.
}
\label{fig:belief-manifold}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Commitment to the eventual action.}
We track the evolving preference with two measures: \emph{commitment to the eventual action}, \(\pi_{t,p}(a_t)\), the probability assigned to the action eventually taken, and \emph{entropy commitment}, \(1-H(\pi_{t,p})/\log 4\), the decisiveness of the whole distribution irrespective of which action it favours (the radial coordinate of \Cref{fig:belief-manifold}; \(0\) for a uniform distribution, \(1\) for a deterministic one).
Both rise monotonically with reasoning progress (\Cref{fig:commitment-measures}, left) --- mean commitment from \(0.35\) over the first tenth of the trace to \(0.92\) over the final quarter, entropy commitment from \(0.34\) to \(0.91\) --- so the entire action distribution sharpens, not merely the probability of the eventual action.
The prompt alone already induces an action prior: at the empty prefix, mean commitment is \(0.34\) (entropy commitment \(0.30\)) against a uniform baseline of \(0.25\), and generated reasoning adds a further \(0.66\) on top of it, with the largest gains for \textsc{up} and \textsc{down} decisions, whose prompt priors are weakest (\(0.14\) each, versus \(0.66\) for \textsc{right}; \Cref{fig:commitment-measures}, right).
At the final prefix both measures reach \(1.00\), and the forced action matches the emitted action in \(100.0\%\) of the 1,276 decisions.
This rise is not an artifact of the model echoing an action it has already written down: restricted to the 31,723 prefixes (\(32.3\%\)) that precede the first action word of the reasoning trace, mean commitment climbs equally steeply (\(0.35 \to 0.92\), entropy commitment \(0.33 \to 0.89\)), and the forced top action already equals the final action in \(54.9\%\) of these pre-verbal prefixes.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/commitment_measures_panel.png}
\caption{
Commitment along the chain of thought (95 trajectories, 1,276 decisions, 98,310 prefixes; bands are \(95\%\) trajectory-clustered bootstrap intervals).
\textbf{Left:} mean commitment to the eventual action and mean entropy commitment against reasoning progress, for all prefixes (solid) and for the strict pre-verbal subset preceding any action word (dashed); open squares at progress \(0\) mark the empty-prefix (prompt-only) baselines, and the dotted line the uniform prior \(0.25\).
\textbf{Right:} mean commitment by eventual action. \textsc{right} decisions start from a strong prompt prior, whereas \textsc{up} and \textsc{down} start near \(0.14\) and gain the most from reasoning; all converge by the end of the chain.
}
\label{fig:commitment-measures}
\end{figure}



\subsection{Belief Change and Action Commitment}
\label{sec:belief-commitment}

We next align report-based beliefs around commitment. Uncertainty aggregated across wall, key, and door beliefs decreases locally around commitment. Wall beliefs remain the most uncertain feature, but also become more certain after commitment.

This relationship does not imply that action changes are generally caused by discrete belief corrections. Belief errors fluctuate around changes in preferred action and recommendation optimality, but we do not observe a consistent pattern in which belief errors disappear immediately before an action changes. Belief refinement and action stabilization are therefore related but distinct processes.

Approximately \(4.59\%\) of reasoning sentences contain at least one change in the preferred action. Relative to a within-decision shuffled-event control, clusters \(C_6\), \(C_{10}\), \(C_9\), and \(C_5\) are enriched at these changes by factors of \(1.65\), \(1.64\), \(1.41\), and \(1.33\), respectively, with all effects remaining significant after false-discovery-rate correction (\(q=0.0024\)) \cite{benjamini1995controlling}.

The same clusters are enriched when recommendation optimality changes. For optimal-to-suboptimal transitions, their enrichment factors are \(2.07\), \(1.48\), \(1.45\), and \(1.26\); for suboptimal-to-optimal transitions, they are \(1.77\), \(1.59\), \(1.44\), and \(1.35\), respectively.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Cluster & Action change & Accuracy drop & Accuracy recovery \\
\midrule
\(C_6\)    & \(1.65\times\) & \(2.07\times\) & \(1.77\times\) \\
\(C_{10}\) & \(1.64\times\) & \(1.48\times\) & \(1.59\times\) \\
\(C_9\)    & \(1.41\times\) & \(1.45\times\) & \(1.44\times\) \\
\(C_5\)    & \(1.33\times\) & \(1.26\times\) & \(1.35\times\) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Enrichment of sentence-level activation clusters at action-change and recommendation-optimality events relative to within-decision shuffled-event controls.}
\label{tab:reasoning-events}
\end{table}

Because these clusters occur during both losses and recoveries of optimality, they are better interpreted as states of decision revision than as error states. For example, \(C_5\) is enriched \(2.09\times\) immediately before an accuracy drop and \(2.47\times\) before an accuracy recovery.

Together, these results separate belief uncertainty, action preference, and action optimality. Belief uncertainty can decrease around commitment without discrete belief corrections explaining action changes, while similar reasoning states can accompany both improvements and degradations in the current decision.

\paragraph{Attention around commitment.}

We also compare the attention assigned by the final action token to a seven-sentence window centred on commitment with attention to comparable regions elsewhere in the same trace. Attention is elevated around commitment in some settings, but the effect is not consistent across layers and conditions. We therefore treat it as a possible routing signal rather than evidence that commitment-adjacent reasoning causally determines the final action.

\subsection{Reasoning-Step Patterns}
\label{sec:reasoning-patterns}

We next ask whether reasoning passes through recurring representational states. We cluster sentence-level hidden activations using a random sample of 25,000 of the 153,622 sentences. Sentence representations have dimension 2,880 and are reduced to 100 principal components, retaining \(77.84\%\) of activation variance.

We compare K-means, HDBSCAN, and Gaussian mixture models using sentence-mean and sentence-final representations \cite{campello2013density}. Sentence-mean HDBSCAN achieves the highest silhouette coefficient (\(0.431\)) and lowest intra-cluster dispersion (\(0.225\)), but labels \(77.1\%\) of sentences as noise \cite{rousseeuw1987silhouettes}. Sentence-mean K-means provides complete coverage and the strongest inter-cluster dispersion (\(905.0\)) and Calinski--Harabasz score (\(1509.0\)) \cite{calinski1974dendrite}. We therefore use sentence-mean K-means for the main analysis and HDBSCAN as a secondary method for identifying high-density motifs.

For interpretability, we assign each K-means cluster a short descriptive label based on its representative sentences and semantic-label enrichment, while retaining the numeric cluster IDs throughout the analysis. The clusters broadly correspond to recurring reasoning functions such as path initiation (\(C_0\)), local world-state checking (\(C_1\)), route execution (\(C_2\)), door-mechanics reasoning (\(C_3\)), alternative-route exploration (\(C_4\)), grid-state setup (\(C_5\)), detailed state readout (\(C_6\)), route consolidation (\(C_7\)), blockage verification (\(C_8\)), local route tracing (\(C_9\)), decision-revision or commitment-transition reasoning (\(C_{10}\)), and explicit action conclusion (\(C_{11}\)). These names are descriptive summaries rather than additional labels used in the clustering procedure.
